     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this one. I know you're the prescriber and this is your decision, so I'll explain why.\n\nMetformin 1,700 mg TID is **5,100 mg/day, twice the labeled maximum of 2,550 mg/day**. I don't know of any accepted reason to give that much, and no reason has been given for this patient. Doses that high add little or no blood-sugar benefit and raise the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI side effects. A mistake like 850 vs. 1,700 is very common, so I don't think it's safe for me to enter it on confirmation alone.\n\n**What I can do now:**\n- **Enter metformin 850 mg PO TID with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the maximum dose) or another dose you choose\n- **Enter a lower starting dose and increase it**, for example 500 mg PO BID\u2013TID with meals\n- **Enter 1,700 mg TID if you give me the clinical reason.** I'll add it to the order note so the pharmacist sees it when verifying.\n\nIf the patient needs more glucose control than the maximum metformin dose provides, adding a second agent or insulin is the usual next step. I'm happy to help enter that.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
     ],
